Justin Florentine: As usual.

33
00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:43.620
Justin Florentine: Okay, so we can get started, I guess, it's 3 after,

34
00:04:44.290 --> 00:05:00.209
Justin Florentine: The area of focus today is really to kind of brainstorm what we concluded last session, which was… Maybe we should… Maybe we should use the ordering of top of block

35
00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:07.930
Justin Florentine: To enforce, or prioritize, rather, trustable key revealers.

36
00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:13.170
Justin Florentine: So, the underlying concept was that

37
00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:22.079
Justin Florentine: You know, if you have a track record of not revealing your key, then you don't get to go to the top of the queue.

38
00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:36.390
Justin Florentine: Right? You gotta go to the bottom of the top of block. This still allows you to front run anybody that's in the remainder of the block, but not people that use more trustworthy key providers.

39
00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:40.439
Justin Florentine: So I think that's the core of the mechanic.

40
00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:48.380
Justin Florentine: And Jannik kind of rocks, and opened a PR into Lucid.

41
00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:53.790
Justin Florentine: Which details this.

42
00:05:55.370 --> 00:06:01.989
Justin Florentine: And I apologize, this is the first time I'm seeing this, so I actually have not read this, I'm kind of scanning it right now.

43
00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:09.880
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, sorry about that. I posted it a bit on short notice. You posted the link in the chat as well.

44
00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:13.359
Justin Florentine: Sir, never apologize for solving problems for us, okay?

45
00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:22.079
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, but yeah, maybe I can give a quick overview, overview, if that's helpful.

46
00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:34.230
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, as you said, basically this mechanism of, getting a priority list in, and, like, I think in the last call, we still… or I was at least still calling it whitelist. I think priority list fits better, because it's now…

47
00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:43.479
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, just a priority in the block, and it's still managed by the valid dataset that votes for it on the execution layer.

48
00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:54.249
Jannik Luhn: I think the PR, there's two… can basically split up into two groups, for people to review it. One is the voting contract itself, like, what happens on the…

49
00:06:54.290 --> 00:07:12.670
Jannik Luhn: on the execution layer, and how the changes then propagate into the consensus layer and be turned into this priority list. And then the second part is how this priority list is then, yeah, interpreted or abided by the consensus layer and by the block ordering rules.

50
00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:20.820
Jannik Luhn: So, the second one is just in… the first part is just in addition to the, to Lucid, and the second part is changes to Lucid.

51
00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:35.450
Jannik Luhn: That's why I'm a bit more comfortable with the first part, because that's, yeah, the second part was for me a bit more difficult to, to get my head around, and there might be more, issues with that.

52
00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:56.809
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, basically the mechanism did not change too much, the contract did not change too much from what we discussed last time. The only thing I added is a queuing mechanism to basically decouple the changes that go into the contract, and what gets then executed on the consensus layer.

53
00:07:57.250 --> 00:08:05.480
Jannik Luhn: To prevent, yeah, spamming the content-stayer and to limiting the amount of changes that need to be.

54
00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:15.540
Jannik Luhn: need to be executed on the consensus layer. And also, I added a fee mechanism to, yeah, to discourage spam as well.

55
00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:31.209
Jannik Luhn: And both mechanisms are more or less copied from similar EIPs. They have similar problems, like, for example, I think the validator consolidation messages use something like that, and there are a couple of other proposals that

56
00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:36.080
Jannik Luhn: use similar mechanisms. So that's basically the same idea there as well.

57
00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:42.109
Jannik Luhn: And for the changes to Lucid.

58
00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:56.840
Jannik Luhn: one big one is that key messages, I think, now have to be signed, so I added a signature. The reason is that in plain Lucid, it doesn't matter, like, as long as the key is valid, we are happy. But now, with the priority list.

59
00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:07.769
Jannik Luhn: You could impersonate a key publisher on the priority list, and just pretend your transaction, or the key came from one of them, and then get a…

60
00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:11.580
Jannik Luhn: gonna place in that, yeah, priority part of the block.

61
00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:19.610
Jannik Luhn: And that would be a problem. And to prevent that, we can either sign key messages, which I think is easiest.

62
00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:27.380
Jannik Luhn: The other alternative would be to assign the tickets, but I think that's a bit more complicated from a…

63
00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:39.850
Jannik Luhn: key publisher's perspective operationally, because they have to review the… or the transactions have to be routed through them, basically, whereas the key release is already what they have to do, so I think signing them is…

64
00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:41.590
Jannik Luhn: Quite natural there.

65
00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:42.740
Jannik Luhn: Okay.

66
00:09:45.740 --> 00:10:03.710
Jannik Luhn: The other big change is that I removed the max preceding commitments, for now at least, because I didn't find a better way. I think it's quite difficult to combine that with the priority list mechanism. Both are kind of there to, yeah, to

67
00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:08.249
Jannik Luhn: to constrain the order of transactions in a block and combining both is a bit tricky.

68
00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:11.500
Jannik Luhn: Okay.

69
00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:14.030
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, I'm not sure if I should go…

70
00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:29.560
Jannik Luhn: a bit more into detail there. Basically, Lucid has this recovery mechanism, so if a key publi- or if a- if a builder does not publish the payload, or does- publishes an- publishes an invalid payload, then in the next block, it will kind of…

71
00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:38.839
Jannik Luhn: make sure that the transactions that were committed still get into the next block. And I extended this to…

72
00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:46.860
Jannik Luhn: Also reorder the transactions in case the builder releases a

73
00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:56.880
Jannik Luhn: A payload that is, yeah, that has the transactions ordered in the wrong way, that does not, fulfill the priority list ordering requirement.

74
00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:12.009
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, and basically to resolve this, they get reordered in this recovery mechanism step, and this is now difficult for, the… basically the key publishers, which want to

75
00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:32.270
Jannik Luhn: ensure that the max preceding commitments are fulfilled would have to predict this reordering step, potentially. So they have to look at all the transactions in the block and potentially reorder them, but they can't actually do this at the moment, because the key publisher is not specified in the commitment itself. It's only in the ticket.

76
00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:33.650
Jannik Luhn: So…

77
00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:51.920
Jannik Luhn: Maybe there are other ways to resolve it, but for me, I think the easiest way was to just replace or remove this max preceding commitments mechanism, especially because I think now it's not really needed anymore, or at least not as crucial because we have this other ordering mechanism. But yeah, it would be interesting.

78
00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:52.570
Justin Florentine: Yeah.

79
00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:54.479
Jannik Luhn: Get feedback from Lucid authors.

80
00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.330
Justin Florentine: I find this…

81
00:11:56.740 --> 00:12:08.800
Justin Florentine: aesthetically pleasing? I think max preceding commitments feels a little bit overloaded to me, and ambiguous, and what you're replacing with is a lot clearer and easier to follow.

82
00:12:10.020 --> 00:12:14.579
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, I think so too, so… Yeah.

83
00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:23.940
Jannik Luhn: I think that's the big changes, I'm not sure if there's too much else,

84
00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:28.180
Jannik Luhn: But yeah, it would be really helpful for me to get feedback from,

85
00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:33.069
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, you, Justin, or other authors of Lucid.

86
00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:42.599
Jannik Luhn: One thing I did not have a look at yet is the engine API changes, which I think there is at least one necessary to, yeah, to make the, the…

87
00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:46.370
Jannik Luhn: Priority list, to communicate the priority list,

88
00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:57.490
Jannik Luhn: But the protocol itself, I think, is… should work like this, but would be interesting to know if there's issues with it, or if other… if things could be done better.

89
00:12:58.160 --> 00:12:58.970
Justin Florentine: Okay.

90
00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:16.530
Justin Florentine: I'll make a note to pay particular attention to the Engine API changes, thank you. But yes, I will review this entire thing, hopefully today. I'll also try to get some of Ander's attention, at least, and maybe Julian.

91
00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:19.670
Justin Florentine: To take a look at it.

92
00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:23.230
Justin Florentine: I'm also going to add a

93
00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:27.530
Justin Florentine: commit to this. Janak, I'm going to put you on the authors.

94
00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:34.420
Justin Florentine: List, if we end up pulling this in, because I think this is a pretty big contribution.

95
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:44.829
Justin Florentine: If… if we proceed with this, it's gonna sort out the optionality issue. And I'm also gonna escalate this to Tama.

96
00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:52.990
Justin Florentine: He had, some concerns about optionality in general, and I want to make sure that this is addressing those.

97
00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:58.689
Justin Florentine: Sorry, I'm just saying that out loud as action items for me.

98
00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.760
Jannik Luhn: Cool, yeah, thank you, that's very helpful.

99
00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:18.619
Justin Florentine: So, the… let's see here, the body of people that are voting on the priority list of key publishers.

100
00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:23.859
Justin Florentine: Is that going to be all attesters? A subset of attesters?

101
00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:32.129
Justin Florentine: How big of a body do we need to vote on whether or not keys were revealed correctly?

102
00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:42.489
Jannik Luhn: Right now, in the proposal, it's just the set of all active validators, weighted by, effective balance.

103
00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:43.240
Justin Florentine: Right.

104
00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:50.550
Jannik Luhn: That might be overdoing it, maybe a smaller subset would also be good enough.

105
00:14:51.890 --> 00:14:55.620
Jannik Luhn: But I'm not sure if that would actually make things easier.

106
00:14:56.580 --> 00:14:57.460
Jannik Luhn: Oh.

107
00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:00.380
Justin Florentine: Okay.

108
00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:07.280
Justin Florentine: Maybe I'll talk to some CL folks about that. I guess the contention would be…

109
00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:14.719
Justin Florentine: I think the first alternative that pops to mind would be limiting it to people that already have duties in the EPIC.

110
00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:23.950
Justin Florentine: Like, maybe you don't need the entire validator set to vote on it, but everybody that is, you know…

111
00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:25.640
Justin Florentine: Umm.

112
00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:28.519
Justin Florentine: The testing in the EPIC could.

113
00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:38.059
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, yeah, this might make sense. I think the main argument for that would be efficiency.

114
00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:45.810
Jannik Luhn: Right, because, I mean, the validators don't vote continuously, they just, delegate to a.

115
00:15:46.690 --> 00:16:03.679
Jannik Luhn: Basically, they only have to update it when they think that the key publisher set needs to be exchanged. So, I think the main argument for restricting the set is making the counting or the tallying of the vote more efficient.

116
00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:15.550
Jannik Luhn: And that is like I tried to do it, try to, structure it in a way that you can compute it iteratively. The.

117
00:16:16.450 --> 00:16:22.320
Jannik Luhn: The tally, so you don't have to recompute it whenever there's a change in the contract.

118
00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:29.539
Jannik Luhn: But you still need to recompute it when the effective balances change, which is once per epoch.

119
00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:44.080
Jannik Luhn: And I don't think there's a way around that, because that's basically the voting weights. So if that turns out to be too, yeah, too computationally heavy, then it might make sense to restrict that.

120
00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:52.549
Jannik Luhn: I did some preliminary benchmarks, and they look good to me, so I'm not sure if it's necessary.

121
00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:53.740
Jannik Luhn: Oh.

122
00:16:54.000 --> 00:17:03.360
Jannik Luhn: But yeah, something to consider. I don't think… I don't think the full validator set is, is strictly necessary for, to make it secure, so…

123
00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:10.159
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, using just a subset would probably also be fine.

124
00:17:12.099 --> 00:17:12.959
Justin Florentine: Okay.

125
00:17:20.419 --> 00:17:22.199
Justin Florentine: Well, it's an open floor.

126
00:17:22.369 --> 00:17:25.809
Justin Florentine: This is kind of it for the agenda for today, so…

127
00:17:26.469 --> 00:17:36.179
Justin Florentine: If folks want to review this PR, and… Ask questions, comments, concerns… Floor is yours.

128
00:17:41.999 --> 00:17:45.419
Justin Florentine: Oh, do we have a note on, like, testing the keys?

129
00:17:46.489 --> 00:17:51.989
Justin Florentine: I.e, like, if you want to assert that, like, hey, you didn't reveal the right key.

130
00:17:52.889 --> 00:17:58.729
Justin Florentine: Like, how do you know that that's the case? Or are we just saying you didn't reveal a key at all?

131
00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:09.790
Jannik Luhn: I didn't look into this specifically, but that should already be handled by existing Lucid, right? Is there a difference?

132
00:18:11.610 --> 00:18:22.779
Jannik Luhn: And we just… we reject keys that… we just have an additional rejection condition now, if the signature is invalid, but we still reject it if the key itself is invalid, which…

133
00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:25.520
Jannik Luhn: I assume Lucid already does.

134
00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:33.650
Justin Florentine: it… Does… but…

135
00:18:36.230 --> 00:18:41.990
Justin Florentine: I guess what we need to figure out is, like, how that reason gets back from the CL to…

136
00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:47.010
Justin Florentine: The priority list.

137
00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:56.730
Jannik Luhn: Yeah.

138
00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:04.579
Jannik Luhn: I mean, there's no automatic mechanism to kick out key publishers.

139
00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:11.000
Jannik Luhn: At the moment, I'm not sure if that would be desirable.

140
00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:17.969
Jannik Luhn: So they don't get automatically punished for revealing an invalid key.

141
00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:21.030
Justin Florentine: That's a good question.

142
00:19:22.540 --> 00:19:28.770
Justin Florentine: Like, a habitual line stepper is gonna be, like, still at the top, or the bottom of the top of the block.

143
00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:31.949
Justin Florentine: They're still gonna be able to front-run the public pool.

144
00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:40.879
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, it basically relies on the validator set to do their job and to vote them out.

145
00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:57.719
Justin Florentine: I mean, that in itself should be a signal for people to stop using it, but I guess…

146
00:19:59.170 --> 00:20:00.719
Justin Florentine: How much do we trust that?

147
00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:19.309
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, but this could probably be an external software, basically.

148
00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:26.519
Jannik Luhn: A monitor that checks… And that validators are encouraged to run the checks which

149
00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:31.399
Jannik Luhn: Key publishers do their job, and Vote them out if not.

150
00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:42.430
Jannik Luhn: And that could also…

151
00:20:43.660 --> 00:21:00.979
Jannik Luhn: kind of be a way to document a way for new key publishers to join? Because that's also something we discussed last week, that if you're a new key publisher, you can already start your job, you can already start working, just not in this priority section, and then make a name for yourself, and at some point be upgraded.

152
00:21:01.130 --> 00:21:07.719
Jannik Luhn: And maybe this can, at least to some extent, be automated, and if the conditions of that are kind of defined by this.

153
00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:12.209
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, external, tool.

154
00:21:12.510 --> 00:21:14.060
Jannik Luhn: That might be helpful.

155
00:21:22.060 --> 00:21:25.229
Jannik Luhn: I guess it both goes both directions, it's what I'm saying.

156
00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:27.389
Justin Florentine: Right, right, right.

157
00:21:35.940 --> 00:21:40.370
Justin Florentine: Right, permissionless entry to the key provider set.

158
00:21:55.130 --> 00:21:58.680
Jannik Luhn: And there's another thing I,

159
00:22:01.210 --> 00:22:11.759
Jannik Luhn: I want to mention is I said that max preceding commitments is gone, but the top-of-block fee is still there, and I basically left it there because it's…

160
00:22:13.340 --> 00:22:20.559
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, this one doesn't clash with the new mechanism, but I also think it's maybe not as crucial to have it anymore.

161
00:22:21.190 --> 00:22:27.309
Jannik Luhn: Because as long as you're in this priority section of the block, you don't really care where you are, and if you're not in the priority section, you have

162
00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:28.230
Jannik Luhn: Yeah.

163
00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:31.269
Jannik Luhn: Something's lost already, maybe.

164
00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:36.570
Jannik Luhn: So that might also be something we could consider removing.

165
00:22:37.050 --> 00:22:37.770
Justin Florentine: Hmm.

166
00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:46.349
Jannik Luhn: Just to simplify it a little bit.

167
00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:56.530
Justin Florentine: Are you talking about the top of BlockFi? Which fee are you talking about?

168
00:22:56.530 --> 00:22:58.100
Jannik Luhn: Yes, the type of Blockfi.

169
00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:18.970
Justin Florentine: Why is that… why is that safe to do? Because we do need to constrain that to a particular, fraction of the overall block size.

170
00:23:21.460 --> 00:23:24.869
Justin Florentine: And so if it's constrained, we kind of need some way to…

171
00:23:26.140 --> 00:23:29.899
Justin Florentine: Regulate who gets in and out of it, right?

172
00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:40.139
Jannik Luhn: Okay, right, yeah, that's, that's true. It's not just about ordering, it's also just who gets in and who doesn't, if there's…

173
00:23:40.250 --> 00:23:46.870
Jannik Luhn: congestion. Yeah, true. For that, we still need it. But I guess it doesn't… wouldn't have to be necessarily at top of BlockFee.

174
00:23:47.330 --> 00:23:49.850
Jannik Luhn: Because once you're in, you don't care about the order anymore, that's, like.

175
00:23:49.850 --> 00:23:52.120
Justin Florentine: Right.

176
00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:56.399
Justin Florentine: Yeah, and now you have two dimensions to potentially order by, right?

177
00:23:57.390 --> 00:24:02.000
Justin Florentine: I'm willing to pay X, and my key provider is trusted Y.

178
00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:10.970
Jannik Luhn: Oh, okay, that is actually not done by… maybe, maybe that should be done by the proposal,

179
00:24:13.280 --> 00:24:23.019
Jannik Luhn: The… as long as your, key publisher is prioritized, it doesn't matter if you're just barely in, or if you've got 100% of the votes.

180
00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:26.380
Justin Florentine: Right…

181
00:24:27.090 --> 00:24:33.650
Jannik Luhn: That might be interesting to still have, yeah, put the trusted… the most trusted key publishers in the front.

182
00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:41.999
Justin Florentine: But, like, I don't know that we want mev searchers competing on key trust. Like, they need another dimension to compete on, right?

183
00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:44.739
Justin Florentine: And I think that should be…

184
00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:52.350
Justin Florentine: I don't know, it should be… I like the existing mechanism for that, let me say.

185
00:24:52.650 --> 00:24:53.799
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, yeah, true.

186
00:24:55.990 --> 00:24:58.710
Jannik Luhn: That would be very strange if people tried to game that.

187
00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:04.920
Justin Florentine: Right, right, you could get some weird collusion… Effects.

188
00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:10.239
Justin Florentine: Because you're grouping everybody by, like, oh, this is a really trusted key publisher. It's like, okay, well…

189
00:25:11.250 --> 00:25:12.830
Justin Florentine: everybody's using them.

190
00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:17.960
Justin Florentine: How does… how does the ordering get… get decided?

191
00:25:18.620 --> 00:25:19.450
Justin Florentine: Hmm.

192
00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:27.400
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, okay, so maybe keeping the top of BlockFeed probably makes… still makes sense.

193
00:25:29.440 --> 00:25:36.260
Justin Florentine: I think it does, but now I don't know how to actually write an implementation that prioritizes

194
00:25:37.260 --> 00:25:48.330
Justin Florentine: the key publisher. I mean, you could spec it like there's a threshold. Like, anybody that has, you know, fewer than however many demerits on their key publishing.

195
00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:51.909
Justin Florentine: All get to participate in Top of Block equally.

196
00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:58.440
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, this is basically how it works. I mean,

197
00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:08.609
Jannik Luhn: The top of Blockfi is still used right now to order in this priority section of the block.

198
00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:17.190
Jannik Luhn: And in that section, it doesn't matter which key publisher you used, as long as you used one of them that had… that's more than 50% support.

199
00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:18.110
Jannik Luhn: Oh.

200
00:26:18.110 --> 00:26:19.480
Justin Florentine: Okay.

201
00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:31.020
Jannik Luhn: So they're not even ordered by a key publisher anymore. It can be key publisher A at the top of the block, then transaction from another key publisher B, and then key publisher A again.

202
00:26:31.020 --> 00:26:35.819
Justin Florentine: Yeah, they can be interleaved, it's just a set of everybody that has met the trust threshold.

203
00:26:35.820 --> 00:26:36.720
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, exactly.

204
00:26:36.720 --> 00:26:43.859
Justin Florentine: Okay, good, good. That's fine, then. Then we just keep the… the fee. Okay, good.

205
00:26:49.570 --> 00:26:53.320
Justin Florentine: Okay.

206
00:27:02.010 --> 00:27:02.710
Justin Florentine: Hmm.

207
00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:04.890
Justin Florentine: What else we got here?

208
00:27:08.770 --> 00:27:18.079
Jannik Luhn: There is a part which I don't like, syntactically too much, I didn't know how to do it properly, the key message signing, the format of that.

209
00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:21.419
Jannik Luhn: Because it kind of combines.

210
00:27:21.550 --> 00:27:26.960
Jannik Luhn: the normal RLP transaction signing with the…

211
00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:35.119
Jannik Luhn: SSZ container signing, but none really fits, because the container is a,

212
00:27:35.270 --> 00:27:43.749
Jannik Luhn: For container signing, we usually use BLS signatures, but for this we can't, because the key publishers are execution-layer addresses.

213
00:27:45.230 --> 00:27:49.440
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, it's kind of mixing both, so I didn't…

214
00:27:50.070 --> 00:27:53.120
Jannik Luhn: I wasn't sure which mechanism to use, so that's…

215
00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:58.279
Jannik Luhn: There might be better ways to express this, or there definitely are better ways to express this.

216
00:28:02.220 --> 00:28:06.330
Justin Florentine: So frame transactions lets us abstract this a little bit.

217
00:28:08.070 --> 00:28:11.320
Justin Florentine: There might be an opportunity to leverage that.

218
00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:16.830
Jannik Luhn: Yes, but it's the key message, so it's not really a transaction.

219
00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:22.500
Justin Florentine: Oh, right, right, right, right. I have. Yep, yep, yep.

220
00:28:33.940 --> 00:28:38.500
Jannik Luhn: So it's basically a consensus-layer message that uses the execution-layer signing scheme.

221
00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:40.910
Justin Florentine: Right.

222
00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:47.790
Justin Florentine: So, it's a potential interaction with frames, then.

223
00:28:49.530 --> 00:28:57.250
Justin Florentine: Like, if you had… if you had… if you were to treat the validation of The…

224
00:28:57.570 --> 00:29:04.229
Justin Florentine: Origin of the key… As a frame within the transaction.

225
00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:14.879
Justin Florentine: It would need to know how to interact with this key message to retrieve the actual key material, and how big it was, and what type it was.

226
00:29:40.900 --> 00:29:41.710
Justin Florentine: Okay.

227
00:29:45.030 --> 00:29:50.339
Justin Florentine: Alright, we're at the 30-minute mark. Does anybody have anything else they want to go over? Because…

